    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In vertebrates, social status not only impacts reproductive success, but also regulates physiology across the reproductive axis. While lower social status represses aggressive behavior and limits mating opportunities, a dynamic social environment can allow subordinate individuals to “ascend” to dominance. The hormonal, physiological, and behavioral changes that accompany the social ascent process are well understood, but little is known about the mechanisms by which the brain translates the sensory information associated with social opportunity into changes along the reproductive axis. While previous studies have implicated broad regions of the brain in social ascent, none have convincingly identified the neuronal populations involved. Here, we determined the specific brain nuclei and neuronal populations activated upon the perception of social opportunity in the African cichlid fish Astatotilapia burtoni. In the wild, male burtoni exist in one of two social states, dominant (D) or nondominant (ND), and ND males can ascend to dominance in response to a dynamic social environment. This natural social structure allows for reliable, reproducible manipulation of social status in the laboratory. By allowing males to ascend socially and measuring consequent neuronal activity via immediate early gene expression, we determined that multiple discrete regions of the preoptic area of the hypothalamus are activated during social ascent. Furthermore, three candidate peptidergic populations (Gonadotropin Releasing Hormone 1, Arginine Vasotocin, Isotocin) show increased activity in ascending as compared to stable dominant and non-dominant males. We observe Isotocin receptor expression in regions of the brain responsible for both sensory processing and overall arousal, suggesting that Isotocin may play a role in modulating the brain’s response to both visual and olfactory cues during social flux. Finally, we find that Gonadotropin Releasing Hormone 1 neurons, the so- called “gateway to the reproductive axis” express neither Isotocin nor Arginine Vasotocin receptors, suggesting that at least a portion of this mechanism exists external to the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis.",
